WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.210 --> 00:00:05.210
- [Narrator] Sleeping on a plane is hard,

00:00:05.550 --> 00:00:08.070
especially if you're in an economy seat.

00:00:08.070 --> 00:00:10.560
So we asked an expert for advice.

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.960
- I'm an Occupational
Therapist, Industrial Designer,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.820
and Certified Professional Ergonomist.

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:16.860
With seated-sleep posture,

00:00:16.860 --> 00:00:18.690
not only do you fall into
these awkward postures,

00:00:18.690 --> 00:00:22.110
but you're also compressing the vertebrae

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.330
and your spine at the different levels.

00:00:24.330 --> 00:00:25.620
You have to plan ahead.

00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.300
- [Narrator] Here's what
a professional ergonomist

00:00:27.300 --> 00:00:30.810
recommends for sleeping
in an economy plane seat.

00:00:30.810 --> 00:00:34.290
First, you might consider
using the tray table, but.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.600
- The position when using the tray table,

00:00:36.600 --> 00:00:38.610
if you're propping up on your forearms,

00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:40.290
that awkward positioning,

00:00:40.290 --> 00:00:42.270
the contact pressure
against the tray table,

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:43.860
all that's gonna irritate tissue

00:00:43.860 --> 00:00:46.800
and sleep just like it would
during your waking hours.

00:00:46.800 --> 00:00:49.080
- [Narrator] Contact pressure
happens when soft tissues

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:51.510
are pressed against a
hard surface for too long,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.400
slowing blood flow to your extremities,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.560
and often causing numbness or tingling.

00:00:55.560 --> 00:00:57.540
- With soft tissue compression,

00:00:57.540 --> 00:01:00.450
you can compromise circulation.

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:02.670
You can impinge nerves,

00:01:02.670 --> 00:01:04.440
which can lead to numbness or tingling,

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.390
which will often wake people up.

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:07.980
- [Narrator] Then there's your posture.

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.090
- [Carla] It's going to be difficult

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:10.830
for you to maintain that posture very long

00:01:10.830 --> 00:01:13.320
because you will be
experiencing excessive flexion

00:01:13.320 --> 00:01:15.390
at your hips and knees.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:16.890
Your spine will be rounded.

00:01:16.890 --> 00:01:18.960
You'll be putting pressure
through your forearms.

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:19.950
- [Narrator] The solve?

00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.340
- There are both inflatable
and memory foam cushions

00:01:23.340 --> 00:01:26.340
available that start to at least address

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:29.070
some of those risk factors,
such as the contact pressure

00:01:29.070 --> 00:01:30.630
against the hard surface of the tray table

00:01:30.630 --> 00:01:33.420
by providing a soft surface
for your body to rest.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:34.830
They can hold your arms

00:01:34.830 --> 00:01:37.260
and then it also cradles your
head and neck and your face

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:40.380
in a way that attempts to
at least position your head

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:43.260
and neck in some sort
of a neutral posture.

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:44.580
- [Narrator] But depending on your height,

00:01:44.580 --> 00:01:47.490
and how far back the person
in front of you reclines,

00:01:47.490 --> 00:01:50.040
the tray table might
not be your best option.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.170
- I think it's probably
gonna serve you well

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.690
just for sort of a
short stretch for a nap.

00:01:54.690 --> 00:01:57.090
I don't think you'll be getting
the highest quality of sleep

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:58.200
in that position.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:00.750
- [Narrator] So what about
leaning back in your seat?

00:02:00.750 --> 00:02:03.810
- If you have to sleep
in an economy class seat

00:02:03.810 --> 00:02:06.930
in a seated position, you are
up against some challenges.

00:02:06.930 --> 00:02:10.020
Ideal state, your body
would would prefer to sleep

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:12.300
in a fully-supported lying down position.

00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:15.900
Your body already has
to endure weightbearing

00:02:15.900 --> 00:02:18.030
and force through your spine, your hips,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.190
your pelvis, your lower extremities.

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:21.900
Whenever it's sleeping, ideally,

00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:25.590
it is great if you can steal
a couple degrees of recline

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:27.330
from your seatback just to put your body

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:29.430
in a little bit more of a
supported sleep position.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.290
- [Narrator] Just be
mindful of your neighbors.

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:33.630
You can also adjust your back position.

00:02:33.630 --> 00:02:36.630
- Putting just a gentle
cushion behind the seatback

00:02:36.630 --> 00:02:38.850
to support the spine is beneficial.

00:02:38.850 --> 00:02:41.460
The more head and neck
support you can provide,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:44.820
the better chance you have at
achieving some restful sleep.

00:02:44.820 --> 00:02:46.260
- [Narrator] And here's
where a travel pillow

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:47.370
comes in handy.

00:02:47.370 --> 00:02:50.040
- A lot of these supports
are not all created equal.

00:02:50.040 --> 00:02:54.270
There are the tried and
true U-shaped neck pillows.

00:02:54.270 --> 00:02:57.060
You wanna keep your head and
neck as neutral as possible

00:02:57.060 --> 00:03:00.660
against the unfortunate barrier of gravity

00:03:00.660 --> 00:03:02.310
and upright positioning.

00:03:02.310 --> 00:03:05.160
A lot of the ones that I
like wrap around the neck

00:03:05.160 --> 00:03:08.340
and have a support just so
they don't shift during sleep.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.880
They can sort of provide
full neck support,

00:03:11.880 --> 00:03:14.610
so not just forward flexion,

00:03:14.610 --> 00:03:17.520
but lateral flexion, even some extension.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.350
It can hold your head
in like a comfortable,

00:03:19.350 --> 00:03:21.210
yet supported position for sleep.

00:03:21.210 --> 00:03:22.740
- [Narrator] And if you
don't have a pillow,

00:03:22.740 --> 00:03:24.090
as a last resort.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:28.530
- You can try to make do
with layers of clothing.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:29.790
I think the thesis here

00:03:29.790 --> 00:03:32.940
is that if you are in a
scenario where you have to sleep

00:03:32.940 --> 00:03:34.950
or you're flying during evening hours,

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:36.270
you have to plan ahead.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:37.800
- [Narrator] Besides pillows and posture,

00:03:37.800 --> 00:03:41.070
there are a few other helpful
tips for sleeping on planes.

00:03:41.070 --> 00:03:44.190
- You might think about
limiting light exposure

00:03:44.190 --> 00:03:45.420
with an eye mask,

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:49.560
or having layers or a
blanket for thermal comfort,

00:03:49.560 --> 00:03:50.700
noise-canceling headphones

00:03:50.700 --> 00:03:54.060
just to limit some of the
auditory stimulation on a plane.

00:03:54.060 --> 00:03:56.160
- [Narrator] When it comes
to picking between the aisle,

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.840
middle, and window seats,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.360
there isn't one best option for everyone.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:01.710
- It's gonna be a bit subjective

00:04:01.710 --> 00:04:03.450
in terms of what people
find most comfortable

00:04:03.450 --> 00:04:04.800
sleeping on an airplane.

00:04:04.800 --> 00:04:06.120
- [Narrator] If you
need to use the bathroom

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:09.180
or stretch frequently, the
aisle might be your best option,

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:10.860
but it also means you'll be woken up

00:04:10.860 --> 00:04:13.650
every time your seatmates
need to get out as well.

00:04:13.650 --> 00:04:15.930
- I think if you can
avoid the middle seat,

00:04:15.930 --> 00:04:18.960
that's probably the best advice.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.180
- [Narrator] And while the
window seat can provide support,

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:22.440
just make sure to add a pillow

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:24.240
or piece of clothing as a cushion.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:26.490
Importantly, flyers should find a position

00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.200
that allows them to move around a bit

00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:31.050
while they sleep, like
they normally would in bed.

00:04:31.050 --> 00:04:32.940
- The body is built to be dynamic,

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:34.770
and your body needs those movement breaks

00:04:34.770 --> 00:04:36.450
in order to lubricate joints,

00:04:36.450 --> 00:04:40.740
to release muscle tension,
to mitigate strain.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:41.640
- [Narrator] But for those people

00:04:41.640 --> 00:04:44.040
who still really can't snooze mid-flight.

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.180
- It's always worth it to get an upgrade.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.763
(bright music)

